1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,864
Every monsoon, floods in India take lives that a few hours of warning could have saved.

2
00:00:05,864 --> 00:00:10,975
This is JalRakshak, an agentic early-warning and response platform built only on free,

3
00:00:10,975 --> 00:00:13,931
open data and a local language model.

4
00:00:15,233 --> 00:00:19,921
River and rainfall forecasts already exist, but they rarely become clear, local,

5
00:00:19,921 --> 00:00:22,266
multilingual instructions in time.

6
00:00:22,266 --> 00:00:27,303
District officers are overloaded, and citizens get warnings they cannot act on.

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:31,674
The command centre watches twelve flood-prone cities.

8
00:00:31,674 --> 00:00:36,207
For each one we pull Open-Meteo rainfall forecasts and GloFAS river discharge,

9
00:00:36,207 --> 00:00:41,244
and compute a transparent zero-to-one-hundred risk score for the next seventy-two hours.

10
00:00:41,244 --> 00:00:45,371
This is a synthetic replay inspired by Assam's monsoon floods.

11
00:00:46,267 --> 00:00:47,973
Let's open Guwahati.

12
00:00:47,973 --> 00:00:52,986
The score combines heavy-rain categories from the India Meteorological Department,

13
00:00:52,986 --> 00:00:58,959
the forecast Brahmaputra discharge against danger thresholds derived from forty years of river data,

14
00:00:58,959 --> 00:01:02,478
soil wetness from recent rain, and ward vulnerability.

15
00:01:02,478 --> 00:01:06,558
Every factor is shown, so an officer can see exactly why.

16
00:01:07,467 --> 00:01:09,426
We drill down to ward level.

17
00:01:09,426 --> 00:01:13,638
Every ward has its own score, and low-lying, vulnerable wards climb first,

18
00:01:13,638 --> 00:01:17,319
with the nearest shelters and hospitals already mapped.

19
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:24,927
As the replay moves forward, rain intensifies and the river crosses its danger mark. The risk turns red.

20
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:28,214
Now the agents take over.

21
00:01:28,214 --> 00:01:32,177
A monitor agent, running on a small open model through Ollama,

22
00:01:32,177 --> 00:01:35,658
reasons over the data and decides an alert is needed.

23
00:01:35,658 --> 00:01:38,710
The drafter writes SMS and WhatsApp messages.

24
00:01:38,710 --> 00:01:43,477
Guardrails check length, script, and that every number matches verified data.

25
00:01:43,477 --> 00:01:48,885
The evacuation planner routes people to safe shelters, and a situation report is drafted.

26
00:01:48,885 --> 00:01:52,659
Every step is logged with its reasoning and inputs.

27
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,996
Nothing goes out without a human.

28
00:01:56,996 --> 00:02:03,595
The officer reviews the alert in English, Hindi, Marathi, Tamil and Bengali, edits if needed, and approves.

29
00:02:03,595 --> 00:02:07,988
Only then is it dispatched, and that decision is logged too.

30
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:11,647
The agents also write a situation report.

31
00:02:11,647 --> 00:02:16,918
The district officer exports it as a PDF in one click, ready for the control room.

32
00:02:17,833 --> 00:02:21,815
Citizens get a light page that works on low-end phones and offline.

33
00:02:21,815 --> 00:02:25,898
Enter a place to see your risk, what to do now, your nearest shelter,

34
00:02:25,898 --> 00:02:28,869
and emergency numbers like one-one-two.

35
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:35,577
It runs on a laptop with one command, needs no paid services or keys,

36
00:02:35,577 --> 00:02:44,496
and uses open source throughout: Open-Meteo, OpenStreetMap, Leaflet, OSRM, Ollama, FastAPI, React and LightGBM.

37
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:50,391
JalRakshak turns open forecasts into warnings people can act on.

38
00:02:50,391 --> 00:02:55,141
Built by Madhav Zanwar and Divyani Papalkar from PCCoE Pune.

